From the three demos I've refined the rule: there's a "key" object (the only object of its color — largest such when several), and somewhere two objects share the same shape (same orientation, or rotated). The twin to recolor is the one **outside the key object's bounding box** (in demo 2 the vertical 0-domino and in demo 3 the 3-T lie inside the key's bbox and are left alone; their partners get the key's color). When both twins are outside (demo 1), the nearer one to the key is recolored. Pairs with different colors take priority when several twin pairs exist.

Applying this to the test: key = the 2-shaped L (largest single-object color), and the twin pair is the 3×2 block of 8s (rows 5–7) vs the 2×3 block of 8s (rows 9–10, which sits inside the key's bounding box). So the 3×2 block gets recolored to 2.

