I've now worked out a rule that fits all four demos: group objects by bounding-box size; if a group (of ≥2 objects) contains "sparse" members (fewer cells than its bounding box), recolor the sparse one (the one farthest from the key object if several) to the key object's color (the largest singleton-color object); otherwise fall back to exact-shape twin pairs, recoloring the twin farther from the key. This reproduces every demo (demo 1: the far L-tromino twin; demo 2: the vertical 8-domino; demo 3: the far T; demo 4: the sparse S-tet in a 2×3 box). On the test grid the key is the 2-shaped L, and the twin 8-blocks (3×2 at rows 5-7 vs 2×3 at rows 9-10) form a rotation pair with no sparse group, so the block farther from the key — the 3×2 block — is recolored to 2.

