After re-analyzing all five demos with fresh eyes, I found the consistent pattern: there is a needle object shaped like an L-corner (corner cell + two perpendicular arms; in the demos it's the unique object of its color). The changed object always lies in the **quadrant opposite to both arms** (diagonally behind the needle's corner): up-right in demo 1, up-left in demo 2, down-left in demo 3, up-left in demo 4, up-right in demo 5 — all exactly the away-quadrant of their needles.

In the test grid the needle is the 2-colored L (corner at row 6, col 12, arms pointing right and down), so the away-quadrant is up-left (rows < 6, cols < 12). The only object fully inside that quadrant is the 0-colored blob (rows 1-3, cols 7-9) — the 8-block at rows 5-7 straddles the corner row and is excluded. This also agrees with the same-shape-twin view: the 0-blob and the bottom-right 8-shape are the same shape, and the twin farther from the needle (the 0-blob) gets recolored — consistent with the near twin having already failed. So I recolor the 0-blob to 2.

